From: <fill in your name/email>
Date: Fri, 07 Aug 2026 08:10:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Vulkan: Don't mutate environ when VK_ICD_FILENAMES already matches

ScopedVkLoaderEnvironment::setICDEnvironment() calls setenv() on
VK_ICD_FILENAMES every time a Vulkan instance is probed or created, and
the destructor calls setenv()/unsetenv() again to restore the old value.
setenv() and unsetenv() are MT-Unsafe -- the glibc manual annotates both
"MT-Unsafe const:env" and states plainly that "Modifications of
environment variables are not allowed in multi-threaded programs".
getenv() is only "MT-Safe env", i.e. safe against other readers, not
against a concurrent writer: glibc's setenv() takes a lock that getenv()
does not.

ANGLE reaches this code from three production paths (vk_renderer.cpp,
gpu_info_util/SystemInfo_vulkan.cpp, DeviceVk.cpp), all of which run
long after the embedding application has started threads, so each
set/restore pair is a race window against every getenv() in the process
-- including getenv() calls made from inside other libraries.

Observed as a startup SIGSEGV in Chromium 151.0.7922.71 on Debian
bookworm arm64 with --single-process, at a rate of ~7% under launch
concurrency (100% with a race amplifier). --single-process puts the GPU
thread in the browser process, so the ANGLE ICD probe on
Chrome_InProcGpuThread runs concurrently with Chromium's async fontconfig
init, which is inside FcInitLoadConfigAndFonts -> FcConfigGetFilename ->
getenv() on a thread-pool worker. The faulting instruction is the glibc
environ walk "ldr x19, [x20, #8]!" with x19 holding recycled-allocator
garbage rather than a char *.

On glibc <= 2.40 the failure is a use-after-free rather than a torn read.
__add_to_environ() takes the "name not present" path, which does
realloc(last_environ, (size + 2) * sizeof (char *)) and republishes the
result as __environ. When realloc moves the block the old array is freed
while another thread is walking it, and that thread dereferences freed
memory. glibc 2.41 (commit 7a61e7f557a9, "stdlib: Make getenv
thread-safe in more cases") stopped deallocating old environ arrays, so
the use-after-free is confined to glibc <= 2.40 -- but the call remains
MT-Unsafe by contract and must not depend on the libc version.

Whether the realloc moves the block depends on the size class of the
current environ array, i.e. on how many variables the parent process
exported. Measured, with the move/no-move observed directly via a setenv
interposer: an sh parent (16 vars) grew in place and never crashed
(0/6); a python3 parent (17 vars, CPython adds LC_CTYPE per PEP 538)
moved the array and always crashed (6/6). One extra exported variable
flips the outcome, which is why this looks like an unreproducible flake.

A lock inside ANGLE cannot fix this, because the racing reader is
usually not ANGLE code. That approach was already tried and rejected in
Chromium (crrev.com/c/7725326, abandoned: "There's no reasonable way to
make these functions thread-safe. The pointer returned from getenv() can
easily be invalidated."). Renaming the variable to VK_DRIVER_FILES would
not help either -- it is still a setenv() -- and was reverted once
already for breaking the Fuchsia loader (crrev.com/c/3522821, reverted
by crrev.com/c/3580980).

ANGLE is a library and cannot choose when its embedder creates threads,
so the environment has to be correct before ANGLE is reached. Chromium
adopted exactly this pattern for its own env writes in
crrev.com/c/7725187 ("set env FC_FONTATIONS and EGL_PLATFORM early ...
avoids race conditions issues of setenv/getenv"), which moved a setenv()
out of GlobalFontConfig construction -- but it did not cover ANGLE's
VK_ICD_FILENAMES write, which is the writer that remains.

This CL makes that contract usable: when VK_ICD_FILENAMES already holds
exactly the value setICDEnvironment() would write, skip the mutation and
report success. mChangedICDEnv stays false, which also suppresses the
paired restore in the destructor, so a process that pre-set the variable
before creating threads performs zero environ mutations on this path.
Behaviour is unchanged when the variable is unset or holds a different
value.

This is intentionally minimal and does not remove the setenv() for
embedders that do not pre-seed. The longer-term fix is to stop selecting
an ICD through the environment at all: VK_LUNARG_direct_driver_loading
passes the driver to vkCreateInstance via VkDirectDriverLoadingInfoLUNARG
with no environ involvement. That needs Vulkan loader >= 1.3.234 and
ANGLE loading the ICD itself, so it is not a drop-in given the loader
versions ANGLE still supports.

Bug: angleproject:543664586
Test: Chromium 151.0.7922.71, Debian bookworm arm64, --single-process,
    launched under concurrency from a 17-variable parent environment.
    Baseline ~7% SIGSEGV in getenv() under FcConfigGetFilename; 100%
    with an LD_PRELOAD getenv() that sleeps 20ms mid-walk for
    FONTCONFIG*/XDG_*/HOME/FC_* names. With VK_ICD_FILENAMES pre-set to
    the bundled SwiftShader ICD path before exec, a setenv interposer
    shows this call site performing no environ mutation at all and the
    crash does not occur: 0/15 and 0/60 amplified, 0/936 natural.
    Also clean: --disable-software-rasterizer (0/15) and --use-gl=disabled
    (0/15), both of which skip the ICD probe entirely. NOT a fix, for the
    record: --no-zygote looks clean naturally (0/936) but is 15/15 under
    the amplifier -- it only narrows the window.
    NOT COMPILE-TESTED -- see the notes below the diff.

---
Generated against angle/angle main @ 7368784df4242e2a98b3820c95a99bd09f6f2fda
(https://chromium.googlesource.com/angle/angle, fetched 2026-08-07)
Apply with:  git am angle-setenv-race.patch     (or: git apply)

diff --git a/src/common/vulkan/vulkan_icd.cpp b/src/common/vulkan/vulkan_icd.cpp
index 9606208..c2076b1 100644
--- a/src/common/vulkan/vulkan_icd.cpp
+++ b/src/common/vulkan/vulkan_icd.cpp
@@ -212,7 +212,30 @@ bool ScopedVkLoaderEnvironment::setICDEnvironment(const char *icd)
     // Override environment variable to use built Mock ICD
     // ANGLE_VK_ICD_JSON gets set to the built mock ICD in BUILD.gn
     mPreviousICDEnv = angle::GetEnvironmentVar(kLoaderICDFilenamesEnv);
-    mChangedICDEnv  = angle::SetEnvironmentVar(kLoaderICDFilenamesEnv, icd);
+
+    // setenv()/unsetenv() are MT-Unsafe ("MT-Unsafe const:env" in the glibc manual): they
+    // mutate the process-wide environ array, which races against any concurrent getenv().
+    // The racing reader does not have to be ANGLE code - it can live in the Vulkan loader,
+    // in fontconfig, or in libc itself, so a lock inside ANGLE cannot make this safe.  On
+    // glibc < 2.41, adding a name that is not already present reallocates the environ array
+    // and frees the old one, so a concurrent getenv() walking that array dereferences freed
+    // memory and crashes.
+    //
+    // ANGLE is a library and cannot control when its embedder creates threads, so the
+    // environment must already be correct by the time we get here.  Embedders that
+    // initialize ANGLE after threads exist (for example Chromium with --single-process or
+    // --in-process-gpu, where the GPU thread runs in the browser process) are expected to
+    // set VK_ICD_FILENAMES before creating any thread.  When the variable already holds the
+    // value we would write, skip the mutation entirely: the loader will do what we want and
+    // no race window is opened.  Leaving mChangedICDEnv false also suppresses the paired
+    // restore in the destructor, which is a second mutation of the same array.
+    if (mPreviousICDEnv.valid() && mPreviousICDEnv.value() == icd)
+    {
+        mChangedICDEnv = false;
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    mChangedICDEnv = angle::SetEnvironmentVar(kLoaderICDFilenamesEnv, icd);
 
     if (!mChangedICDEnv)
     {
-- 

================================================================================
REVIEWER NOTES -- what was and was NOT verified
================================================================================

NOT VERIFIED (no ANGLE build in the environment that produced this patch):

  * Not compile-tested. ANGLE was not built. A reviewer must confirm the
    change builds on every platform that compiles vulkan_icd.cpp, including
    the !ANGLE_PLATFORM_ANDROID / ANGLE_PLATFORM_MACOS / ANGLE_PLATFORM_FUCHSIA
    permutations, since setICDEnvironment() sits under those guards.
  * No ANGLE test was run (angle_end2end_tests, angle_unittests, or the
    VulkanHelper path in src/tests/test_utils/VulkanHelper.cpp:219).
  * Behaviour with ICD::Mock was reasoned about but not exercised; the Mock
    path uses the same setICDEnvironment() and should benefit identically,
    but confirm the mock-ICD tests still select the mock driver.

VERIFIED:

  * The diff applies cleanly to angle/angle main at the SHA above
    (git apply --check).
  * The changed logic was extracted into a standalone translation unit built
    against ANGLE's real src/common/Optional.h and compiled with
    g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra. It type-checks, and all three cases behave
    as intended:
      - VK_ICD_FILENAMES unset          -> setenv() called, mChangedICDEnv=1
                                           (destructor restores, as today)
      - set to the SAME value           -> NO setenv(), mChangedICDEnv=0
                                           (destructor does not restore),
                                           returns true, mICD unchanged
      - set to a DIFFERENT value        -> setenv() called, as today
  * Optional<std::string>::valid()/value() semantics were read from
    src/common/Optional.h; note GetEnvironmentVar() returns an empty string
    for an unset variable, so the comparison cannot spuriously match (the ICD
    path passed in is never empty -- it is a concatenated module path).

POINTS A REVIEWER SHOULD PUSH ON:

  * This narrows the race to the not-pre-seeded case rather than eliminating
    setenv() from the path. If ANGLE wants the stronger guarantee, the two
    other environ writers in this file (VK_LAYER_PATH via
    PrependPathToEnvironmentVar under mEnableDebugLayers, and NODEVICE_SELECT
    under MSan/ASan) deserve the same treatment; they were left alone here
    to keep the change minimal and because neither is on the default
    production path.
  * crrev.com/c/7699542 (open) proposes unsetting VK_INSTANCE_LAYERS inside
    ScopedVkLoaderEnvironment for an unrelated MangoHud crash. That would add
    two more environ mutations to this exact hot path and would make this race
    worse; the two changes should be looked at together.
